-- The HYDRATION exhibit for the DMN exporter (§4.4 of
-- `specs/todo/DMN-EXPORT-PROGRAM-MODEL-SPEC.md`), and the one DMN golden that is
-- driven through BOTH engines as a MustPass.
--
-- `sumtype.l4` cannot be that file and never will be: it exists to exhibit what
-- the exporter REFUSES, and `stated term` -- a payload projection on a
-- user-declared union -- is D-SUMTYPE Blocking by R4-a, which stays. KIE cannot
-- compile the L4 text that refusal emits, so sumtype.dmn can only ever be a
-- MustFail. This file carries the measurement instead, on EMITTER-PRODUCED XML,
-- which is strictly stronger than the hand-written probe in
-- jl4/tests-cli/fixtures/dmn-hydration-probe/: that one pins the IDIOM, this one
-- pins that we emit it.
--
-- Everything here must stay engine-clean. Nothing in this file may be D-SUMTYPE
-- Blocking, because a single unloadable decision takes the whole verdict with
-- it.
--
-- What each part is for:
--
--   * `Applicant`               a record with THREE computed fields, and they
--                               are declared in an order that is NOT
--                               topological: `band` is declared BEFORE
--                               `total income`, which it reads. A boxed
--                               context's entries evaluate in order and only
--                               EARLIER siblings are in scope, so an exporter
--                               that emitted them in declaration order would
--                               emit `band` reading a name that is not yet
--                               bound -- and FEEL answers null rather than
--                               failing. jl4/examples/ok/computed-fields.l4
--                               cannot catch that, because ITS declaration
--                               order already happens to be topological. This
--                               one is the test.
--   * `applicant score`         reads a computed field by PROJECTION (`x's f`),
--                               which is L4 house style;
--   * `applicant band width`    reads one by APPLICATION (`f x`), which is what
--                               jl4/examples/legal/regcf/regcf-wizard.l4
--                               writes. Both spellings fold, because both occur
--                               in real corpora and recognising only one would
--                               make the other silently worse.
--                               ITS BINDER IS ALSO SPELLED `applicant`, AND
--                               THAT IS DELIBERATE, not the oversight
--                               sumtype.l4:161-163 warns against. Two GIVENs of
--                               one name fold to one inputData name, so the
--                               model carries `input_applicant` +
--                               `input_applicant_2` and TWO hydrators of ONE
--                               record type -- which is what exercises the
--                               hydrated-itemDefinition name reuse and the
--                               `applicant_hydrated_2` uniquifier. The price is
--                               that this file's fidelity report opens with
--                               D-SCOPE and D-RENAME (both Lossy) before the
--                               notes it exists to demonstrate. Paid on
--                               purpose; sumtype.l4 avoids the same collision
--                               because there it would buy nothing;
--   * `the leading applicant`   a record-valued DECISION, read through a
--                               computed field by `leading total`. That is the
--                               RequiredDecision branch of hydration: a
--                               hydrator's source need not be an inputData, and
--                               without this the branch would be dead code. It
--                               PROJECTS an applicant rather than constructing
--                               one, for the reason given at its definition;
--   * `capped bonus`            GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER. Since R8-d' this
--                               tabulates, with the absent case as the
--                               <defaultOutputEntry>, which is FEEL `null`.
--                               MEASURED on both engines before the lowering
--                               was written -- see
--                               jl4/tests-cli/fixtures/dmn-null-probe/;
--   * `bonus is settled`        `isJust`, recognised by the PRELUDE's own
--     `bonus is unsettled`      Unique, and `isNothing` likewise. Rendered
--                               `!= null` and `= null`;
--   * `bonus or zero`           a CONSIDER over a MAYBE, rendered
--                               `if .. != null then .. else ..`. The third
--                               absence-testing route, and the only one that
--                               composes inside a larger expression.
--
-- Read the emitted DRG for the shape H1 buys: `applicant score` has NO direct
-- edge to `applicant`, only one to the hydrator, because the emitted FEEL no
-- longer names `applicant` at all. That is R11 -- the graph says what the
-- expression says -- and it is a consequence of the fold rather than a
-- decoration on top of it.
--
-- Figures are illustrative; the point is the LOWERING, not the policy.

IMPORT prelude

§ `Hydration`

-- The computed fields are declared in the order `band`, `total income`,
-- `band width` ON PURPOSE. `band` reads `total income`, so declaration order is
-- NOT a legal context-entry order and the exporter must sort.
DECLARE Applicant
  HAS `salary`        IS A NUMBER
      `other income`  IS A NUMBER
      `band`          IS A NUMBER
        MEANS IF   `total income` AT LEAST 100000
              THEN 3
              ELSE IF   `total income` AT LEAST 50000
                   THEN 2
                   ELSE 1
      `total income`  IS A NUMBER
        MEANS `salary` PLUS `other income`
      `band width`    IS A NUMBER
        MEANS `band` TIMES 25000

-- The PROJECTION spelling of a computed read.
GIVEN applicant IS AN Applicant
GIVETH A NUMBER
`applicant score` applicant MEANS
  applicant's `total income` DIVIDED BY 1000

-- The APPLICATION spelling of the same thing. Both fold to a path access on the
-- hydrator; neither leaves an L4 call in the artifact.
GIVEN applicant IS AN Applicant
GIVETH A NUMBER
`applicant band width` applicant MEANS
  `band width` applicant

-- A record-valued DECISION, so that a hydrator's source is not always an
-- inputData and the RequiredDecision branch is not dead code.
--
-- It reads the applicant out of an enclosing record rather than CONSTRUCTING
-- one, and that is forced rather than stylistic: `Applicant WITH salary IS ..`
-- is an L4 named-argument application, which renderFeelIn refuses verbatim on
-- purpose (a FEEL context literal drops the constructor tag, skips reserved-word
-- checking, and -- the reason that matters here -- omits computed fields while
-- Proj still reads them). One verbatim decision would make this whole file
-- unloadable, which is exactly the trap this exhibit exists to stay out of.
--
-- It is also NULLARY, which is what makes it an instance at all: hydration keys
-- on a nullary reference, because that is the only thing whose FEEL name a
-- context entry can read. A unary `f submission` is a call, and a computed read
-- through one falls to the graceful refusal below.
DECLARE Submission
  HAS `primary`   IS AN Applicant
      `secondary` IS AN Applicant

ASSUME `the submission` IS A Submission

GIVETH AN Applicant
`the leading applicant` MEANS
  `the submission`'s `primary`

GIVETH A NUMBER
`leading total` MEANS
  `the leading applicant`'s `total income`

-- R8-d'. The value channel: a table whose absent case is a null default output.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`capped bonus` n MEANS
  IF n AT MOST 5000 THEN JUST n ELSE NOTHING

-- R8-d'. The combinator table, both halves.
GIVEN b IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`bonus is settled` b MEANS
  isJust b

GIVEN c IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`bonus is unsettled` c MEANS
  isNothing c

-- R8-d'. The CONSIDER route, which is the one that composes.
GIVEN m IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`bonus or zero` m MEANS
  CONSIDER m
  WHEN JUST x  THEN x
  WHEN NOTHING THEN 0
